 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed changes are pushed over self-hosted WebSockets to deliver fast updates without recurring realtime vendor fees or provider coupling, accepting the operational work of managing connection capacity and reconnect/catch-up flows across the change store, delivery, and browser sync. Five-second HTTPS polling (simpler stateless operation, but repeated reads and multi-second lag) and a hosted realtime service (lower connection-ops burden, but recurring cost and provider coupling) were rejected. See [Change Delivery Mechanism](decisions/change-delivery.md).
